{"text": "\u2018NewroToxicotogy 7 (1):197-206 (1986)\nCopyright \u00a9 1986 by Into ress, Ie\n\nBrain Trace Elements in Alzheimer's Disease\n\nW.D. EHMANN!, W.R. MARKESBERY?, M, ALAUDDIN', T..M, HOSSAIN!\nAND E.H. BRUBAKER!\n\nDepartments of \"Chemistry, #Pathology, ?Newrology and ?Sanders Brown\nResearch Center on Aging, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY\n40506\n\nABSTRACT: Instrumental neutron activation analysis has been\nused to determine the concentrations of 16 elements in selected\nbrain regions and separated gray- and white-matter specimens\ntrom histologically verified Alzheimer's oisease (AD) and age-\nmatched control patients. Significantly different (p < 0.05)\n\u2018mean concentrations of Br, Cl, Cs, Hg, N, Na, P, and Rb were\nobserved in AD buik brain samples compared to controls, while\n1 significant differences were observed for Ag, Co, Cr, Fe. K,\nSb, Sc, and Se. The differences that are most persistent and\nlargest in magnitude for the pooled bulk samples, males and\nfemales, loft and right hemispheres, and separated gray and\nwhite matter are the elevation of Br and Hg and the depletion of\nRb in AD compared to controls. Significant interelement\nCorrelations forthe later elements in both AD and control brains\nare also documented. Based on these studies, the possibilly of\nan etiological role for trace elements in AD clearly deserves\nfurther investigation\n\nKey Words: Trace Elements, Alzhoimer's Disease, Brain, Instrumental Neutron\n\nActivation Analysis\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nDementing disorders are a major\ninternational health problem and Alzheimer\u2019s\ndisease (AD) is recognized as the most\ncommon cause of dementia, The etiology of\nAD is not known. An etiologic hypothesis\nthat has received considerable recent attention\nrelates to the possible role of trace-element\ntoxicity in AD. Aluminum (Al) has been\nimplicated as a potential neurotoxic factor in\n\nAD by Crapper era, (1973; 1976) and Trapp\ntal, (1978), who reported a higher incidence\nof elevated Al values in AD brains. However,\nMeDermott et al. (1979) and Markesbery ef\na. 1981; 1983), found no differences in bulk\nbrain levels for Al for contol and AD patents,\nbut id demonstrate increased Al concentrations\ninthe brains of both control and AD patients\nwith increasing age. At the cellular level, Pel\nand Brody (1980) have reported Al enrichment\nthe nucle of neurofibrillary tangle (NFT)-\nbearing neurons compared to normal neurons\n\nPease sond requests for eprint to Dr.\nLoxington, Kentucky 40506,\n\n1. Ehmann, Depanment of Chemistry, Univesity of Kentucky,\n\nPresent adcresses: M, Alauddin, Departnontof Chomisty, Wagner College, Stata Istane, New York 10901\nTM. Houssaln, Eastman Kodak Compaiy, Rochester, New York 14850; \u20ac.H, Braker. arkansas Eastman\n\nKodak Company, Batesvite, Arkansas 72501\n\n50574017",
